A||N||6||darkmagic||headliner repair||||1||
Z||000000||darkmagic||07-10-2001||11:50 PM||||my car is suffering from the drooping headliner syndrome!<P>can anybody walk me through how to fix this.. or should i just go and replace the whole thing?<P>anyone know a place in the toronto area that does this kind of repair?||||reg||1
Z||000001||DecadenceR||07-11-2001||01:06 AM||||If it's coming down, you might as well replace it.<BR>You can try to fix it, but it's just going to come back pretty soon.<BR>Spray glue is no good because it soaks through the fabric and sometimes looks like somoene got excited all over your headliner. Plus you're just gluing bad foam to itself again. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Torma||07-11-2001||02:45 AM||||3M has some good spray adhesive, but it may soak through the material so...I agree...Replace it!<P>It is easy to do anyway...<BR>My favorite catalog, JC whitney sells the headliner carpet by the yard--cheap.  Pep Boys sells it too I think.<P>Take down all the plastic stuff and the headliner<P>Get some of the not-so-safe for the the enviroment(non-latex) contact cement...used for formica countertops.<P>Peel off the old headliner materal.  Some foam sticks to the board so you'll have to rub it off with your hand or whatever.<P>Some people brush fiberglass resin on the board before the cement, but I didn't.  I just brushed on the cement, heavily.  When it got tacky I layed the material on and smoothed it out.  Cut out some holes and reinstall.<P>This is my solution.  Oh, leave your windows open.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  It will smell for a few days.<P>Joe<p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||07-11-2001||02:49 AM||||Go to this thread:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006609.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006609.html</A> <P>It describes exactly how to do it and I posted pics of the process. It takes about 2 hours to do.<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">     <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 07-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||07-11-2001||08:07 AM||||To hold you until you get time to remove the headliner for recovering get upholstery twist pins from WalMart (in the fabric department, about $1.87 for a package of 10).  They are like buttons with a corkscrew shaped pin.  Screw it up into the headliner board to hold the fabric up.  Place them in a pattern so they look like they belong.<p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 07-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||07-11-2001||11:56 AM||||after I pull of the cloth, I rub it gently with wire tire brush. Takes almost all the foam residue off. I just redid my already replaced one in my car with black leather over the top of it. The contact glue has held for 3 years and its so good the fabric wouldnt even try to pull back off. Made nice padding for the leather. Im a drive with windows always open guy, so the light tan was filthy from 3 years, and all the cleaners I tried-even steam- left a mess of water stains. point is you may want to cover it with something else while you have it out...vinyl or some other fabric.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||07-11-2001||12:31 PM||||3M FoamFast74 spray adhesive is made for foam backed fabrics.<P>It sprays on like a spider web of silly string and does not wet the fabric.  You spray both surfaces and then join them.  You'll have about 10 minutes to get the wrinkles out before the glue starts to set.<P>I've had pretty good luck with this glue so far.||||reg||1
